Delegate one `focusout` listener on `#deck` (capture), covering BOTH `.morePop` and `[data-rail="plus"]`; defer one frame and re-read `document.activeElement` rather than trusting `relatedTarget`; and run the module's own teardown, never a bare DOM edit:

```js
var inMore = function (el) {
  return !!(el && el.closest &&
    (el.closest(".morePop") || el.closest('[data-rail="plus"]')));
};
deck.addEventListener("focusout", function (event) {
  if (!moreOpen || !inMore(event.target)) return;
  /* paintRail() destroys the focused plus on the way IN, firing a focusout
     with relatedTarget null that is indistinguishable from a Shift+Tab out.
     So settle a frame and ask where focus actually landed. */
  requestAnimationFrame(function () {
    if (!moreOpen) return;
    var a = document.activeElement;
    if (!a || a === document.body || inMore(a)) return; /* body = outside click / window blur; the click handler owns that */
    moreOpen = false;
    paintRail();
    say("More, closed.");
  });
}, true);
```

`moreOpen = false; paintRail()` is the load-bearing correction — it keeps the flag and the DOM in sync, so the next press of More still opens it. `paintRail()` will not steal focus here: I measured `document.activeElement === BODY` at every one of these focusout events, so its `was.closest(".rail")` guard (line 9601) returns null and it re-focuses nothing, leaving the browser to land focus on `.projSwitch` unimpeded. Driven with this shape: menu stays open on open; Tab x4 closes it with focus correctly on `.projSwitch` and `aria-expanded="false"`; Tab INTO the menu keeps it open; Escape still closes to the plus; the next press of More opens on the FIRST press. Add the `say("More, closed.")` so the close is announced the way Escape's is — silent closure is the same class as the r1 `orientation-switch-says-nothing-on-desk` finding.
